p.src:
The stop-loss example is one of the lecture’s few explicit numerical illustrations. If a stock trades at $P_0 = 100$ and the investor places a stop-loss order at $P_{\text{stop}} = 80,$ then the intended cap on loss is roughly $\frac{100-80}{100}=0.20.$ In trigger language, $\begin{aligned}
\text{sell stop triggers when } & P \le P_{\text{stop}}, \\
\text{buy stop triggers when } & P \ge P_{\text{stop}}.\end{aligned}$ The buy stop is the natural order for a short seller who fears an adverse upward move.

